source: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Te1CwrbBO2o
yield: one large rösti — feeds 2 hungry, or 3-4 as a side
ingredients:
  - item: potato
    mass: 1000
    basis: true
    note: any potato works, but a starchy/floury one (agria etc) gives a better result
  - item: white onion
    note: very thinly sliced. classic amount is half an onion, but one whole small onion is about right in practice — don't go heavier
  - item: clarified butter
    note: generous — enough to pool and bubble visibly up the sides throughout the cook, not just a films worth
  - item: neutral oil
    note: a splash, alongside the clarified butter
  - item: salt
    note: season the potato heavily — potato takes salt well, don't be shy — plus a little more over the onion
  - item: black pepper
    note: heavy, over the onion layer
steps:
  - peel and grate the potatoes — box grater normally, though a food processor's grating attachment works identically, since the outside caramelises over the cook and hides how it was cut
  - squeeze out as much water as possible — bundle the grated potato in a clean tea towel and wring hard, or press it against the side of a sink using your body weight. expect to push out nearly a pint of water from a kilo. the drier the potato going in, the crisper and deeper-caramelised the result
  - season the squeezed potato generously with salt and mix through
  - if not already clarified, microwave a block of butter ~1 min, let it sit until the milk solids settle out, then use only the clear fat — the solids scorch into black flecks over the long slow cook otherwise
  - heat a pan with a generous layer of clarified butter plus a splash of oil in the bottom
  - pack a layer of potato in, focusing on building up the edges rather than the centre — the centre fills in on its own as you go
  - lay the sliced onion in the middle only, breaking it up gently — kept separate like this it steams soft and sweet rather than caramelising at a different rate to the potato around it
  - season the onion with a heavy hit of pepper and a little more salt
  - a touch more clarified butter directly over the onion, to help conduct heat through it
  - cap with more potato, mounding it into a dome to fully enclose the onion
  - onto the heat — after 3-4 mins, use a thin flexible spatula (a fish slice) to tuck and round the edges in, compacting the rösti and rounding off its shape
  - keep adding butter around the edges as it cooks — you want to see fat bubbling visibly up the sides continuously. this is what browns the sides evenly; skip it and you get a browned top and bottom with pale, unbrowned sides
  - after the first 8-10 mins on a firmer heat, drop to the lowest heat you have and let it cook through slowly — a full kilo-sized rösti can take up to 45 mins total. this slow cook is what gives the deep caramelised crust and a fully cooked starchy centre, unlike a hash brown's minute or so in the pan
  - to flip — take the pan off the heat first (there's a lot of hot butter), turn the rösti over carefully, lower it back into the pan, then tuck and push the edges back into shape
  - cook the second side the same way
  - give both sides a final crisp-up over higher heat just before serving if needed
  - lift out onto a wire rack, not a plate or tray — stops the bottom going soggy from trapped steam
  - serve with fried eggs
notes: |
  Traditional Swiss dish, and the whole appeal is that the ingredient list is this short —
  potato, butter, half an onion, most of which is already sitting in the cupboard.

  Squeezing the water out is the single biggest lever here. That water, released into the
  pan as steam, is what turns a rösti into a wet soggy mess instead of a crisp one — get rid
  of it and you get deep colour instead.

  Clarified butter over whole butter is a genuine finesse point, not a necessity. Milk solids
  scorch over a 30-45 min cook and speckle the crust black; it won't ruin the dish left in,
  but it's an easy thing to avoid.

  Keeping the onion in the centre rather than mixed through the potato is deliberate — onion
  and potato caramelise at different rates, so mixed through you'd get one over- or
  under-done relative to the other. Sealed inside, the onion steams instead: soft and sweet,
  while the potato around it does the caramelising.

  Fat bubbling up the sides throughout the cook is essential, not optional — it's the only
  way the sides brown at the same rate as the top and bottom.

  Smaller and thinner cooks faster, but a full-size rösti is more of a showstopper and the
  long slow cook is what gives it a deeper, cakier interior than a fried hash brown ever gets.

  Rest on a rack, not a plate or tray — otherwise steam gets trapped underneath and softens
  the crust you just spent 40 minutes building.
